    "question": {
      ":question/id": "aaa2eb96-4c21-5faf-ab2b-38c5e40c1939",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3} &=-8 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-6(\\frac{5}{3})=-8.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-8-(-10)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{5}{3}$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(2)-(\\frac{5}{3})=4.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=4-(2)-(- \\frac{5}{3})=\\frac{11}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{11}{3}$, ${x}_{2}=2$, and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{5}{3}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
