    "question": {
      ":question/id": "808c7a0d-22f5-5147-9ea4-e1e63782552b",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y-z &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y+2z &=0 \\\\ z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that $z=1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+2(1)=0.\n$$\n\n$$\ny=0-(2)=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y=-2$ and $z=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(-2)-(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}-(2)-(-1)=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=- \\frac{1}{2}$, $y=-2$, and $z=1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨- \\frac{1}{2},-2,1⟩.\n$$",
