    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2e5aa4cd-5c4d-5d74-b056-4b9b518ae72b",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-1)=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=1-(-1)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(2)-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1-(-2)-(1)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=2$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨2,2,-1⟩.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
