    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1d632d3c-97c0-51f4-b1b9-243022fd9097",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=- \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-2)=- \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=- \\frac{3}{2}-(-2)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=-2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{1}{2})+(-2)=-3.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-3-(-1)-(-2)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=-2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨0,\\frac{1}{2},-2⟩.\n$$",
